ence is part of what  makes it appealing  and a big part of what i do is trying to  create the simulation models that  replicate these imperfections  going back to the vinyl record player  we could make a regular cd player  a sound as appealing as a final record  player if we add  the required signal processing in order  to match the  sonic characteristics and how do we  start modeling  analog synthesizers first let's take a  quick look  on how they are architected so a typical  analog synthesizer  is composed by different blocks or  modules  they can be oscillators filters  amplifiers and other modulators so  basically you can think about them as  being  analog computers and these analog  computers perform  additions multiplications comparisons  integration and we can say that they  even perform  trigonometric operations here you have  two pictures of  of two different systems this one  you can see that it has a panel where  where  the different modules are laid out  so we have oscillators filters etc we  have a keyboard  and then we have this section that  allows us to  change the internal connections of the  components  on the right hand side we have a fully  modular synthesizer  and you can see that it is composed by  different blocks and each of these  blocks is independent and can be  replaced and you have all the controls  but you can also connect in  in any way you want each of these  modules you have jacks  and you can use wires to to create the  structure that that you want  the synthesizers can be a bit expensive  especially the modular ones where you  get  every module separated and the cost of a  module  could be in the range of 60 dollars to a  thousand dollars  and they can also be a bit bulky and  heavy because we have to put them inside  a case so they are not very  practical to travel with them one way  that we can overcome these problems is  by using a virtual synthesizer  for example vcv rack which is a  software that is free and open source  and