ules i usually start  my analysis with the schematic like in  this case this is a filter  and i start by identifying the different  blocks and components  and at the same time i'm developing a  board that i use  for actually making measurements like  this one  sometimes i simply make the circuit  into a breadboard once i have identified  all the components  i start making the modeling system  modular  since the model provides a wide variety  of components  that can be used in my case could be  electrical components for example  resistors capacitors etc  but one of the things that that i really  really like about  using system modular is that i can  create my own components  it is often the case in other simulators  that  a component is not provided and there is  no easy way of integrating it or  creating it  or sometimes the the way that you create  the components it's kind of convoluted  over the years i have created a library  of components  i have a small version here and you can  see that i have  typical components resistors capacitors  variables  variable resistors and operational  amplifiers  and these are the components that i use  to create the circuits  now let's start on analyzing a simple  circuit  in this case we're going to see the  salinki  filter topology we're going to focus on  the low pass configuration  and this is the circuit you can see that  it uses two resistors  and two capacitors and it has an  operational amplifier  which is connected as a buffer and from  these equations  we can see that in order to define the  cutoff frequency  we need to either change  the resistors of the or the capacitors  and  if we make the two resistors the same  value and the two capacitors this gets  simplified and we can simply  use a pair of variable resistors in  order to define  the cutoff in system modular i have  already connected all the components  into the salinkey filter configuration  you can see two resistors two capacitors  and one buffer but these re